  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 PM CDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nMostly sunny skies, warm temperatures, and breezy southerly \nwinds have been observed across the local area this afternoon.\nWith steady southerly winds expected to continue into the\nevening and overnight hours, a repeat of some patchy dense fog \nis not expected for Friday morning.\n\nAs the pressure gradient continues to tighten across the region\non Friday ahead of the upper level trough to our west, expect\na more windy day across the area with gusts of 45 MPH+ not out \nof the question. At the same time, mid/upper level clouds will \ncontinue to be on the increase, but with weak warm advection \ncontinuing across the area, these clouds will have little impact\non the very pleasant temps we have been enjoying lately. In \nfact, it may be another 1-2F warmer during the afternoon hours \ntomorrow based on temperatures aloft than today. \n\nExpect the main upper level low across southern California to \nlift north into the broader trough of low pressure over the next\n24 hours with a secondary area of low pressure eventually \nhelping to push the broader trough east and into the desert \nsouthwest by Saturday. This low will then lift northeast into \nthe Rockies in concert with the upper level trough later in the\nweekend, increasing chances for precipitation across the local \narea. This progression is just a bit slower than the models were\nhinting at yesterday, and as was noted by the previous shift, \nprecipitation chances for the central and eastern parts of the \narea may now not really increase until Sunday evening or Monday \nmorning. Given the slower progression, a sharper cutoff in QPF \ncan also be noted in the latest forecast, with areas west of HWY\n281 now forecast to receive 1-2+ inches of rainfall, and areas \nfurther east receiving more modest amounts over the period of \n0.5-1\" of precip. While the exact amounts are not set in stone, \nthe heavier amounts are definitely favored to fall over western \nareas, which arguably probably need it the most.\n\nAt the surface, expect a cold front to stall across the local\narea on Saturday, helping to eventually bring temperatures back\ndown closer to climatology by early next week as the main upper\nlevel trough exits the region. As the trough exits to the east,\na more zonal pattern will set up aloft across the plains with\nmainly dry and seasonal weather returning to the local area\nthrough the following weekend.",
